    {
      "@id": "processes/process/actor-role-administration.bpmn#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "history",
      "cat-harness:section": "comment before Collaboration_ActorRoleAdministration",
      "prov:value": "WHY THIS IS NOT COVERED BY `code-change-review.bpmn`, although every step here ends as a reviewed edit to a JSON file. Measured 2026-09-20 across 26 actors, 31 roles and 34 diagrams, the join is CLEAN: 31 of 31 roles bind a lane, no actor names a role that does not exist, no lane binds nothing. So this is not a broken reference — it is a missing process. `admin` holds two permissions no other actor has, `role-management` and `admin-settings`, and it appears in seventeen diagrams as programme-manager, publication-manager, editor, author, reviewer and code-reviewer. THE ONE THING THAT ACTOR UNIQUELY IS WAS THE ONE THING NEVER DRAWN.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "processes/process/actor-role-administration.bpmn#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "comment before Collaboration_ActorRoleAdministration",
      "prov:value": "THE ROLE WAITED FOR AN ANSWER. `administrator` did not exist until 2026-09-20, when the owner answered that administration IS a swimlane. `role-model.md` records inventing a role to make a diagram drawable as the closure-widening failure, so drawing this earlier would have been the error, not the initiative.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-09-20"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "processes/process/actor-role-administration.bpmn#provenance-4",
